{"type":"api-request","sessionId":"c1e11076-829e-4b02-9641-6b48b095d475","id":"115117a5-79d4-4d9e-bd35-a7f2b15861a2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:15:44.132Z","version":"2.1.317","querySource":"repl_main_thread","shapeHash":"abdbf879f1c8baf4d3d130a8ccbe0672e594508c87565d961c1f493173127f1f","params":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","betas":["claude-code-20250219","oauth-2025-04-20","interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14","context-management-2025-06-27","prompt-caching-scope-2026-01-05","per-turn-control-2026-07-01","mid-conversation-tool-changes-2026-07-01","inline-tools-2026-09-15","advisor-tool-2026-03-01","thinking-token-count-2026-05-13","advanced-tool-use-2025-11-20","mid-conversation-system-clear-at-2026-08-21","effort-2025-11-24","dangerous-tool-use-2026-09-03","thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01","thinking-display-updates-2026-08-18","afk-mode-2026-01-31","extended-cache-ttl-2025-04-11","cache-diagnosis-2026-04-07","message-threads-2026-08-12"],"max_tokens":128000,"thinking":{"type":"adaptive","display":"updates"},"context_management":{"edits":[{"type":"clear_thinking_20251015","keep":"all"}]},"output_config":{"effort":"medium"}},"base":"9f79fad2-32e1-4c95-b0d9-50aa0cc818ab","keep":221,"tail":["0e907a08143f502125117536f1872ab6f518819cf5f885e76833d6414b24631a","d704c3f5026da35a845a6fb8b488d5f7b0f196bf18ebd9ae7337658812930e48"],"cacheControl":[{"path":["system",1],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h","scope":"global"}},{"path":["system",2],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}},{"path":["messages",222,"content",0],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}}],"requestDigest":"d4406d8e5d85c8e1ae85f95b62847eb17ff8f65bef91cb12a968eadefe62ec73"}
{"parentUuid":"955468e3-09f0-47ed-b5b0-ccd0d787be4e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj8hG82X2RwVoLPgroD9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've drafted a map of every way people touch the commons: `~/tray/touchpoints.html`.\n\n**What's on it:**\n- **The principle: verification, not comments.** The verification chain at the end of every rendering, the colophon as you call it, gives every claim a version, a source and a result. That's what makes dissent clean: someone can say \"this claim, at this commit, rests on this quote, and here's a pinned source that says otherwise\". That then becomes a fork beside our argument, not an overwrite. Information from elsewhere comes in the same way, with its source, rights and terms.\n- **Eleven touchpoints:** read, cite, dissent, bring evidence, answer questions, take a task, contribute code, bring an agent, license data, train a model, and report a vulnerability. For each, the map gives:\n  - whether it exists today, partly, or not at all;\n  - the workflow and how it's reviewed;\n  - what we ask the person to represent.\n- **Your pull-request question, as it stands today:**\n  - Only Descartes has a contributor guide, a short one on its public mirror. Accepted pull requests get folded into the main source by the maintainer. Contributors must have permission to contribute the material, and must not include credentials or private material.\n  - Praxis and this repo have no guide at all.\n  - The review would check four things: tests, a byte-identical rebuild, no forcing a check to pass, and staying small.\n  - I proposed one more representation: an agent-written pull request names its model and prompt digest, the same rule as for claims.\n- **The open questions sit on their touchpoints, ten in all.** Dissent and code have the most. For example: is a reason alone enough to dissent, or does it need a counter-source? Do contributors sign off or sign their commits?\n\nThe comments standard now says the same thing as the map: dissent against a versioned claim beats a free comment. Committed and pushed as `8a62279`. The map is also linked from trail #30.\n\n**The research on your pricing benchmark is back:**\n- **Market size:** spend on what vendors call ESG data was about $2.2B forecast for 2025. Opimas is the only firm that publishes a running series, and its method is proprietary.\n- **The nonprofit share has never been published.** The vendors name NGO, academic and government inputs but give no weights. So the share you'd charge on has to be computed, and that's a research project in itself. It would need each vendor's revenue by product line, which inputs feed which product, and what licence fees vendors actually pay upstream.\n- **CDP is the case in point.** It already sells its data. In June 2026 it announced a split, with Permira investing in a for-profit arm.\n- **Reference prices:**\n  - Reddit has $203.0M in AI data-licensing contracts over two to three years, from its SEC filing.\n  - Wikimedia Enterprise earned $8.3M in 2024–25, from audited accounts.\n  - News Corp's deal with OpenAI is over $250M over five years, as reported by the WSJ from sources.\n\nNone of these figures is pinned yet, so they're on the trail as leads. None goes into a price until it is.\n\nThe map is a draft in the tray. Its design belongs in Praxis, and Praxis has another session's uncommitted work in progress, so I haven't put it there. Should it go in Praxis once that work lands, or straight into the central plan?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1863,"cache_read_input_tokens":269635,"output_tokens":1090,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1863,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"in