":"req_011CgjCQy773BGcxHY49Wzkz","requestRef":"69971b27-f8e2-4181-be85-f4ddbd7bb0a9","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"e23f97fb-d2e2-4ace-8da6-d61999b4eb30","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:04:45.514Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"c1e11076-829e-4b02-9641-6b48b095d475","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/srvo/dev/anthropic-pbc","sessionId":"c1e11076-829e-4b02-9641-6b48b095d475","version":"2.1.317","gitBranch":"ec-ai-corpus","slug":"calm-crunching-lovelace"}
{"parentUuid":"e23f97fb-d2e2-4ace-8da6-d61999b4eb30","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d1fcd60a-2f37-4659-bf29-bdaae1557aa2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ji5SXZYPZzSPy6zAiENxeD","type":"tool_result","content":"## XIc. ETHICAL CAPITAL BUSINESS MODEL\n\n| Model | Key Question(s) | Action Required | Source |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **The 3 Ps** | Which of three value propositions are we competing on? | 1. **Personality**: Pleasant interface, life coaching. 2. **Performance**: Pure alpha. 3. **Permanence**: Legacy/estate/charitable planning. Can’t compete on cost with algorithms. | Ortel |\n| **Financial Adviser as Life Coach** | Are we helping clients become happier people? | Track non-financial goals. New experiences? Play? Close relationships? Finding meaning? Gratitude practice? Money decisions reveal values. | Ortel |\n| **Analyst’s 3 Rights** | Am I asserting essential rights? | 1. Right to ask for information. 2. Right to get information. 3. Right to act on information. Always with client interests first. | Unknown |\n| **When Uncertain: Client First** | What would client want? | Default decision rule when path unclear. Client interests > everything else. | Ortel |\n| **Leverage Amplifies** | Is leverage helping or hurting? | Leverage neither good nor bad. Amplifies effects of underlying asset productivity. Analyze potential changes in cost of leverage (rate increases). | Unknown |\n| **No Discovery Process (Schwager)** | Jumping into strategy without testing if it fits? | Read → experiment → see what clicks → paper trade → small money → sc\n\n*Accuracy and Relevance columns are filled only where the source attributes a score; blank cells are blank in the source.*\n\n---\n\n## XVI. CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT\n\n### After Each Position Exit:\n\n- [ ]  Which mental models proved most relevant?\n- [ ]  Which did I apply? Which did I ignore?\n- [ ]  Did Valentine’s rules help? Which ones?\n- [ ]  Did Carlson’s process prevent mistakes?\n- [ ]  Did Voss fingerprints detect anything?\n- [ ]  Were Napier’s historical lessons applicable?\n- [ ]  Did Schloss’s value discipline apply? Which rules?\n- [ ]  Was I patient or rushed? (Schloss temperament check)\n- [ ]  **What leverage point was decisive? (Meadows)**\n- [ ]  **Was I analyzing the right leverage point or wrong one?**\n465:## XIa. FUNDAMENTAL QUESTIONS (Brakke, Kroijer, Schwager)\n512:## XIb. VALUATION HYGIENE (Damodaran)\n526:## XII. APPLICATION CHECKLISTS\n647:## XIII. MODEL SELECTION QUICK REF (Practical Application)\n664:## XIV. ANTI-PATTERNS (Common Failures to Flag)\n700:## XVI. CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"a75c5f2b-340f-47be-af3d-d59efe328199","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:04:46.478Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## XIc. ETHICAL CAPITAL BUSINESS MODEL\n\n| Model | Key Question(s) | Action Required | Source |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **The 3 Ps** | Which of three value propositions are we competing on? | 1. **Personality**: Pleasant interface, life coaching. 2. **Performance**: Pure alpha. 3. **Permanence**: Legacy/estate/charitable planning. Can’t compete on cost with algorithms. | Ortel |\n| **Financial Adviser as Life Coach** | Are we helping clients become happier people? | Track non-financial goals. New experiences? Play? Close relationships? Finding meaning? Gratitude practice? Money decisions reveal values. | Ortel |\n| **Analyst’s 3 Rights** | Am I asserting essential rights? | 1. Right to ask for information. 2. Right to get information. 3. Right to act on information. Always with client interests first. | Unknown |\n| **When Uncertain: Client First** | What would client want? | Default decision rule when path unclear. Client interests > everything else. | Ortel |\n| **Leverage Amplifies** | Is leverage helping or hurting? | Leverage neither good nor bad. Amplifies effects of underlying asset productivity. Analyze potential changes in cost of leverage (rate increases). | Unknown |\n| **No Discovery Process (Schwager)** | Jumping into strategy without testing if it fits? | Read → experiment → see what clicks → paper trade → small money → sc\n\n*Accuracy and Relevance columns are filled only where the source attributes a score; blank cells are blank in the source.*\n\n---\n\n## XVI. CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT\n\n### After Each Position Exit:\n\n- [ ]  Which mental models proved most relevant?\n- [ ]  Which did I apply? Which did I ignore?\n- [ ]  Did Valentine’s rules help? Which ones?\n- [ ]  Did Carlson’s process prevent mistakes?\n- [ ]  Did Voss fingerprints detect anything?\n- [ ]  Were Napier’s historical lessons applicable?\n- [ ]  Did Schloss’s value discipline apply? Which rules?\n- [ ]  Was I patient or rushed? (Schloss temperament check)\n- [ ]  **What leverage point was decisive? (Meadows)**\n- [ ]  **Was I analyzing the right leverage point or wrong one?**\n465:## XIa. FUNDAMENTAL QUESTIONS (Brakke, Kroijer, Schwager)\n512:## XIb. VALUATION HYGIENE (Damodaran)\n526:## XII. APPLICATION CHECKLISTS\n647:## XIII. MODEL SELECTION QUICK REF (Practical Application)\n664:## XIV. ANTI-PATTERNS (Common Failures to Flag)\n700:## XVI. CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"6e5eb7d9-41a0-43fd-a95d-a5da03e36a6f","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/srvo/dev/anthropic-pbc","root":"/Users/srvo/dev/anthropic-pbc","branch":"ec-ai-corpus","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":5,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":14},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"srvo/anthropic-pbc","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"srvo/anthropic-pbc","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"srvo/anthropic-pbc","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/srvo/dev/anthropic-pbc","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e23f97fb-d2e2-4ace-8da6-d61999b4eb30","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"c1e11076-829e-4b02-9641-6b48b095d475","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/srvo/dev/anthropic-pbc","sessionId":"c1e11076-829e-4b02-9641-6b48b095d475","version":"2.1.317","gitBranch":"ec-ai-corpus","slug":"calm-crunching-lovelace"}
{"parentUuid":"3e2d8e34-f86a-4275-bb6f-614c67c3be51","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d1fcd60a-2f37-4659-bf29-bdaae1557aa2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EZEo9282ZnoYJkeUuW8x6w","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://theshareholdercommons.com\",\n      \"final_url\": \"https://theshareholdercommons.com\",\n      \"title\": \"Creating Guardrails to Protect our Common Interest\",\n      \"description\": \"Free market capitalism has made us healthier and wealthier for more than two centuries. But there is growing recognition of the costs we are paying for short-sighted business decisions that prioritize profits over our natural environment and human needs.\",\n      \"language\": \"en\",\n      \"text\": \"# OPTIMIZING PORTFOLIOS BY PROTECTING THE COMMONS\\n\\n### the shareholder commons ADVances the theory and practice of system-level investing\\n\\nDiversified investment portfolios depend on a healthy economy. Investors should therefore preserve the social and environmental systems that protect and grow the economy and their portfolios.\\n\\n# AN INNOVATIVE APPROACH\\n\\n### System-Level Investing and System Stewardship\\n\\nThe current financial system measures success by alpha, or relative returns compared to a benchmark. This approach limits individual companies to addressing systemic issues only if doing so will improve their own relative financial performance.\\n\\nWithout a shift in approach allowing investors to consider companies’ impacts on beta (i.e. overall market returns), environmental and social resources will continue to be extracted from society and future portfolio returns will be reduced. Read more here.\\n\\n# What we do\\n\\nSystem-level investing continues to grow and evolve through research, innovation, and implementation by individuals, organizations, and practitioners. The Shareholder Commons supports these efforts through:\\n\\n## IDentifying leaders and best practices\\n\\n## FACilitating knowledge sharing and implementation\\n\\n## field building and public advocacy\\n\\n## What We’re Taking Away from Climate Week NYC\\n\\nRecently, TSC joined Climate Week NYC. We were there for conversations, sessions across the field, and a co-hosted System-Level Investing Knowledge Lab gathering with TIIP. It’s impossible to summarize such a busy week, but three themes stood out. First, systemic...\\n\\n## Join the System-Level Conversation\\n\\nProvide Input at Upcoming Knowledge Lab Roundtables Join us for our next virtual System-Level Investing Knowledge Lab Roundtables to move from listening to action following the conclusion of our Knowledge Lab Listening Tour. We’ll dig into three foundational areas of...\\n\\n## What We Heard: System-Level Investing Knowledge Lab Listening Tour\\n\\nLast month we wrapped the Listening Tour for the System-Level Investing Knowledge Lab: a public-good platform designed to curate, create, and convene in support of the theory and practice of system-level investing. Through in-person and virtual roundtables, one-on-one...\\n\\n## Spotlight on Portfolios on the Ballot 2026\\n\\nLooking Back on Four Years of Portfolios on the Ballot Before Portfolios on the Ballot launched in 2023, TSC aimed to show investors who use a company-specific focus that portfolio-wide risk is embedded throughout the economy and that those risks are actionable in...\\n\\nTogether, we can fundamentally transform our financial system. Get involved today.\",\n      \"author\": null,\n      \"published_date\": \"2026-01-29T18:33:16+00:00\",\n      \"latency_ms\": 313.14014999952633,\n      \"format\": \"markdown\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"errors\": []\n}"}]}]},"uuid":"50559ce5-f52f-4304-9f77-cfaaa475304e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:04:46.994Z","toolUseResult":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://theshareholdercommons.com\",\n      \"final_url\": \"https://theshareholdercommons.com\",\n      \"title\": \"Creating Guardrails to Protect our Common Interest\",\n      \"description\": \"Free market capitalism has made us healthier and wealthier for more than two centuries. But there is growing recognition of the costs we are paying for short-sighted business decisions that prioritize profits over our natural environment and human needs.\",\n      \"language\": \"en\",\n      \"text\": \"# OPTIMIZING PORTFOLIOS BY PROTECTING THE COMMONS\\n\\n### the shareholder commons ADVances the theory and practice of system-level investing\\n\\nDiversified investment portfolios depend on a healthy economy. Investors should therefore preserve the social and environmental systems that protect and grow the economy and their portfolios.\\n\\n# AN INNOVATIVE APPROACH\\n\\n### System-Level Investing and System Stewardship\\n\\nThe current financial system measures success by alpha, or relative returns compared to a benchmark. This approach limits individual companies to addressing systemic issues only if doing so will improve their own relative financial performance.\\n\\nWithout a shift in approach allowing investors to consider companies’ impacts on beta (i.e. overall market returns), environmental and social resources will continue to be extracted from society and future portfolio returns will be reduced. Read more here.\\n\\n# What we do\\n\\nSystem-level investing continues to grow and evolve through research, innovation, and implementation by individuals, organizations, and practitioners. The Shareholder Commons supports these efforts through:\\n\\n## IDentifying leaders and best practices\\n\\n## FACilitating knowledge sharing and implementation\\n\\n## field building and public advocacy\\n\\n## What We’re Taking Away from Climate Week NYC\\n\\nRecently, TSC joined Climate Week NYC. We were there for conversations, sessions across the field, and a co-hosted System-Level Investing Knowledge Lab gathering with TIIP. It’s impossible to summarize such a busy week, but three themes stood out. First, systemic...\\n\\n## Join the System-Level Conversation\\n\\nProvide Input at Upcoming Knowledge Lab Roundtables Join us for our next virtual System-Level Investing Knowledge Lab Roundtables to move from listening to action following the conclusion of our Knowledge Lab Listening Tour. We’ll dig into three foundational areas of...\\n\\n## What We Heard: System-Level Investing Knowledge Lab Listening Tour\\n\\nLast month we wrapped the Listening Tour for the System-Level Investing Knowledge Lab: a public-good platform designed to curate, create, and convene in support of the theory and practice of system-level investing. Through in-person and virtual roundtables, one-on-one...\\n\\n## Spotlight on Portfolios on the Ballot 2026\\n\\nLooking Back on Four Years of Portfolios on the Ballot Before Portfolios on the Ballot launched in 2023, TSC aimed to show investors who use a company-specific focus that portfolio-wide risk is embedded throughout the economy and that those risks are actionable in...\\n\\nTogether, we can fundamentally transform our financial system. Get involved today.\",\n      \"author\": null,\n      \"published_date\": \"2026-01-29T18:33:16+00:00\",\n      \"latency_ms\": 313.14014999952633,\n      \"format\": \"markdown\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"errors\": []\n}"}],"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"6e5eb7d9-41a0-43fd-a95d-a5da03e36a6f","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/srvo/dev/anthropic-pbc","root":"/Users/srvo/dev/anthropic-pbc","branch":"ec-ai-corpus","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":5,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":14},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"srvo/anthropic-pbc","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"srvo/anthropic-pbc","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"srvo/anthropic-pbc","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/srvo/dev/anthropic-pbc","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3e2d8e34-f86a-4275-bb6f-614c67c3be51","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"c1e11076-829e-4b02-9641-6b48b095d475","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/srvo/dev/anthropic-pbc","sessionId":"c1e11076-829e-4b02-9641-6b48b095d475","version":"2.1.317","gitBranch":"ec-ai-corpus","slug":"calm-crunching-lovelace"}
